<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-18.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="045912"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#045912" label="[04:59:12]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Lance R. Vick: generated programs tend to become deadends because no one truly understands how they work usually. And if you understood how it worked (by say just reading the Ada code enough to understand it); then you could just write the equivalent in about 2 months of work in straight C or C++ (as the understanding is usually the delay not the throwing code into files)</span></div><div class="line" id="050010"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#050010" label="[05:00:10]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">deesix: well it exists for anyone who needs the files; they are also all available in a git repo for checkout/pull requests/etc</span></div><div class="line" id="055756"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#055756" label="[05:57:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance R. Vick&gt; oriansj: the neat thing about this generator, if you look at the example, is the generated c code adapts the structure and variable names of the original ada code, and can even include the original ada code as comments for each function if desired</span></div><div class="line" id="055800"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#055800" label="[05:58:00]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance R. Vick&gt; which is much better than any of the generation approaches I have seen at least</span></div><div class="line" id="063213"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#063213" label="[06:32:13]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bubbling back up the scheme approach discussed in 2022: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://fossil.irvise.xyz/gnat-bootstrap/forumpost/dcbd9236fce8a004">https://fossil.irvise.xyz/gnat-bootstrap/forumpost/dcbd9236fce8a004</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="071445"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071445" label="[07:14:45]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, deesix: the vibes i am getting are not to rely on the .tar.gz snapshots, as they can be enabled/disabled on a whim, and are intended as a web browsing convenince rather than something to rely on</span></div><div class="line" id="071503"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071503" label="[07:15:03]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and that it would be better for us to generate the .tar.gzs ourselves</span></div><div class="line" id="071533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071533" label="[07:15:33]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think this kind of opens up another can of worms regarding source distribution as to what we find acceptable.</span></div><div class="line" id="071624"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071624" label="[07:16:24]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently we suppose HTTP (non-SSL) distribution as a hard requirement</span></div><div class="line" id="071736"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#071736" label="[07:17:36]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">HTTP distribution being safe because we already know the hashes of all distfiles?</span></div><div class="line" id="071822"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071822" label="[07:18:22]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: yes, that is not limited to HTTP though. it is the de facto (and imho, correct) choice because it is ubiquitous</span></div><div class="line" id="071918"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#071918" label="[07:19:18]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yes, and you can get away with echo and an nc-ish program if you really must</span></div><div class="line" id="071920"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#071920" label="[07:19:20]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Makes sense</span></div><div class="line" id="071940"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#071940" label="[07:19:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, we have intentionally ensured that (for both ease and trust) we have &quot;upstream&quot; HTTP sources available -- yet we bend the rules on this a fair bit -- using mirrors, i think we even had archive.org at one point</span></div><div class="line" id="072054"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072054" label="[07:20:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so i think the question we need to consider more generally is: For those distfiles that must be pre-loaded into the system (i.e. before we have networking+curl available), do we require that they are available from a non-#bootstrappable, HTTP, source?</span></div><div class="line" id="072128"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#072128" label="[07:21:28]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think that's unavoidable. There are too many unreliable upstreams that do things like re-issue distfiles, force-push over git tags, etc. I need to write to the guix people about some of their distfiles not matching their hashes</span></div><div class="line" id="072139"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072139" label="[07:21:39]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or is it sufficient that they are available from a non-#bootstrappable source of any kind, from which the tarball we use is either a) able to be created from or b) on HTTPS?</span></div><div class="line" id="072205"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072205" label="[07:22:05]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i should say, or b) the same identical tarball, just accessed through HTTPS</span></div><div class="line" id="072241"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072241" label="[07:22:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">same thing goes for everything between networking and SSL support in the live-bootstrap chain -- can we just distribute those tarballs ourselves, and let users check later if those tarballs are OK once SSL support is available?</span></div><div class="line" id="072308"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#072308" label="[07:23:08]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from a trusting-trust perspective they're all untrustworthy until you know you've got the right files on disk because you've hashed them with a hashing function that you've bootstrapped, no?</span></div><div class="line" id="072329"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072329" label="[07:23:29]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">100%</span></div><div class="line" id="072340"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072340" label="[07:23:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so that's why i think our limitations at the moment might be a bit artificial</span></div><div class="line" id="072710"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#072710" label="[07:27:10]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think however you get bootstrap dists, they have to come from a stable, ubiquitous protocol. The most reliable thing I can think of is the mirrors traditionally used in the Free Software world - they're more durable than torrents and don't have a SPOF. But they should be mirrors of upstream sdists, ideally, not mirrors of live-bootstrap-hosted sdists - you don't want to be so trusted that you can swap the sdists out on people</span></div><div class="line" id="072843"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#072843" label="[07:28:43]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Our single biggest problem in stagex is unreliability of the many mirrors for all the bootstrap sources. We hardcode hashes, but it is important that we maintain a provenance for the original/official location as the primary mirror, even if we sometimes have to fall back to a secondary mirror.</span></div><div class="line" id="072907"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#072907" label="[07:29:07]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Our hope is that we get enough people to mirror the dists that we can source from the mirrors at random with our fetch.py tool</span></div><div class="line" id="072921"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#072921" label="[07:29:21]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as long as all hashes match what we have hardcoded in our tree, we call it good</span></div><div class="line" id="072949"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072949" label="[07:29:49]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">^ i think this approach makes a lot of sense</span></div><div class="line" id="072957"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#072957" label="[07:29:57]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think the goal would be for the stagex project to host a full mirror of everything, but if say livebootstrap does the same, great</span></div><div class="line" id="073010"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073010" label="[07:30:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we can source from each others independently managed mirrors in addition to upstream</span></div><div class="line" id="073032"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073032" label="[07:30:32]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: is &quot;primary&quot; = &quot;upstream&quot;, or primary = some &quot;official&quot; stagex mirror?</span></div><div class="line" id="073045"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073045" label="[07:30:45]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">What's the stagex project?</span></div><div class="line" id="073051"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073051" label="[07:30:51]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for now, primary will still be upstream, and just fall back to our own mirror</span></div><div class="line" id="073056"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073056" label="[07:30:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gotcha</span></div><div class="line" id="073149"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073149" label="[07:31:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: full source-bootstrapped, reproducible, multi-signed, and container-native toolchain to build all the things.</span></div><div class="line" id="073231"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073231" label="[07:32:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="073234"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073234" label="[07:32:34]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Having the primary mirror be the real upstream, and ideally errors getting noticed and reported sounds good. There is a theoretical risk if a project like live-bootstrap becomes the trusted primary mirror, because then you can switch the dists and it'll be a while before people notice.</span></div><div class="line" id="073246"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073246" label="[07:32:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yeah, we added a toml file with sources to every project, and will incrementally be adding mirrors as we find them</span></div><div class="line" id="073248"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073248" label="[07:32:48]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or host them</span></div><div class="line" id="073300"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073300" label="[07:33:00]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is my concern, i would want some kind of auto-diversification to avoid that jackdk</span></div><div class="line" id="073319"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073319" label="[07:33:19]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/lance/mirrorfetch/packages/bootstrap/stage1/package.toml">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/lance/mirrorfetch/packages/bootstrap/stage1/package.toml</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="073333"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073333" label="[07:33:33]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once we have at least 3 mirrors, I would want to source randomly</span></div><div class="line" id="073429"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073429" label="[07:34:29]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">This also means there's another metric for the &quot;quality&quot; of the bootstrap: how long do you have to go building stuff before you have a checksumming program to check your sources?</span></div><div class="line" id="073519"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073519" label="[07:35:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for us that is &quot;one of the first things once you get to C&quot; :D</span></div><div class="line" id="073547"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073547" label="[07:35:47]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">makes sense. Though some brave soul might write one in one of the hex languages one day</span></div><div class="line" id="073549"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#073549" label="[07:35:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in our case, we download all sources up front with our fetch.py using the python standard library. Various team members have different host distributions</span></div><div class="line" id="073605"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#073605" label="[07:36:05]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not related, but some day I'd like to see a special flash drive that lets you multiplex a first boot sector and boot partition using some physical mechanism. So you could, for example, move a slider to a position labeled, e.g. &quot;x86-64&quot;, &quot;armv7&quot;, &quot;RISC-V&quot;, etc, and when you plug it in it will present the corresponding boot sector and boot partition. You could then populate the non-multiplexed part with almost exclusively source code and</span></div><div class="line" id="073605"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#073605" label="[07:36:05]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">have a true universal system</span></div><div class="line" id="073626"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073626" label="[07:36:26]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator had some &quot;trusted flash drive&quot; project/idea</span></div><div class="line" id="073630"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#073630" label="[07:36:30]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is somewhat similar</span></div><div class="line" id="073809"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073809" label="[07:38:09]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't even think you need crazy mutliplexing stuff for that, just a slider to choose which boot partition to present</span></div><div class="line" id="073847"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#073847" label="[07:38:47]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is that not what multiplexing is?</span></div><div class="line" id="073921"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#073921" label="[07:39:21]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I thought you meant some kind of crazy hybrid format like the cosmoverse's APE executable format</span></div><div class="line" id="073935"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#073935" label="[07:39:35]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh no, I meant basically what you said</span></div><div class="line" id="074010"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074010" label="[07:40:10]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: stagex looks like a cool project. Unfortunately guix is not bootstrappable at the moment - some sdists have different hashes to what guix expects and I can't proceed beyond openssl-1.1.1l because the test suite has certificate time bombs in it</span></div><div class="line" id="074030"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074030" label="[07:40:30]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(I know you don't use guix in stagex, but your readme currently calls it bootstrappable)</span></div><div class="line" id="074044"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074044" label="[07:40:44]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">One of our goals at distrust/stagex is to ensure our source hashes are the same as those doing baremetal pulls via some of the actual &quot;live&quot; builds. That said, we have used stagex to bootstrap all the way up to secure enclaves, some of which actually do http TLS termination and DNS validation in the stagex-built enclaves... so nothing stopping them from being oracles to reproducibily</span></div><div class="line" id="074046"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074046" label="[07:40:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">download archives from various positions on the internet and confirm there is no dns poisioning or bgp attack nastiness.</span></div><div class="line" id="074055"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074055" label="[07:40:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and these enclaves are remotely attestable and managed by quorums of people.</span></div><div class="line" id="074115"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074115" label="[07:41:15]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">By no means should that be the -only- solution, but if it matches everyone elses, helps stack confidence</span></div><div class="line" id="074125"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#074125" label="[07:41:25]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: well, technically with sufficient permissions all time bombs can be worked around, you just have to figure out the right time to set the clock to</span></div><div class="line" id="074202"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074202" label="[07:42:02]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unmush: yeah, and it would be nice if the builds did a faketime or something. But alas, that's not what's in their package set right now</span></div><div class="line" id="074220"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074220" label="[07:42:20]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">We have to libfaketime in a couple places I admit. Not ideal, but works</span></div><div class="line" id="074241"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074241" label="[07:42:41]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sad guix is not currently bootstrappable, but I am happy to call that a temporary bug, given it is by design and has been in the past</span></div><div class="line" id="074248"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074248" label="[07:42:48]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Fair</span></div><div class="line" id="074319"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074319" label="[07:43:19]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I want to be as charitable as I can be to others, given we have build breaking bugs from time to time too.</span></div><div class="line" id="074422"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074422" label="[07:44:22]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah I like the attitude in these spaces</span></div><div class="line" id="074426"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#074426" label="[07:44:26]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hopefully guix is planning on adding faketime usage the next time the packages in question get updated</span></div><div class="line" id="074439"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074439" label="[07:44:39]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I am mainly critical of the distros that have officially stated something as a non-goal.</span></div><div class="line" id="074450"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074450" label="[07:44:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">people should be aware of that when selecting a toolchain</span></div><div class="line" id="074549"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074549" label="[07:45:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guix has done heroic work on bootstrapping so even though they are not 100% reproducible, credit where due. We copied their rust bootstrap path for instance.</span></div><div class="line" id="074650"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074650" label="[07:46:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in our case we actually can't release something that is not reproducible. Our release process won't allow it. That means we won't have ADA or haskell any time soon... And I need ADA to bootstrap coreboot.</span></div><div class="line" id="074717"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#074717" label="[07:47:17]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">heads has network access now, so would be nice to be able to bootstrap from firmware up</span></div><div class="line" id="074720"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074720" label="[07:47:20]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guix is undoubtedly heroic and the most exciting GNU project I've seen in a long time. It's great to see free software at the forefront of something instead of trailing and trying to free users on existing proprietary software</span></div><div class="line" id="075719"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#075719" label="[07:57:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/issues/485">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/issues/485</a>  -- a summary of my thoughts surrounding source distribution, and the changes i think we should make</span></div><div class="line" id="075733"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#075733" label="[07:57:33]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, Googulator: ^</span></div><div class="line" id="075756"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#075756" label="[07:57:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">feel free to say if you think any part is a bad idea, but say why :P</span></div><div class="line" id="141155"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#141155" label="[14:11:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;And I need ADA to bootstrap coreboot.&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="172519"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#172519" label="[17:25:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's mentioned &quot;native graphics initialization&quot; requires ada/gnat, otherwise coreboot does not need ADA</span></div><div class="line" id="172703"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#172703" label="[17:27:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it should be possible to keep coreboot without ada, and let kernel initialize framebuffer which does not need ada then for neither kernel nor coreboot</span></div><div class="line" id="180701"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#180701" label="[18:07:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: reminds me of <a rel="nofollow" href="https://spaf.cerias.purdue.edu/Yucks/V1/msg00096.html">https://spaf.cerias.purdue.edu/Yucks/V1/msg00096.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="185402"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#185402" label="[18:54:02]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Or ditch framebuffer and connect via serial port? At least on machines where that's possible.</span></div><div class="line" id="191137"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191137" label="[19:11:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my understanding coreboot can initialize VGA still, that's good, and kernel can initialize framebuffer if desired</span></div><div class="line" id="191153"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191153" label="[19:11:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, the real concern is UEFI and CSM being phased out</span></div><div class="line" id="191231"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191231" label="[19:12:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is an extremely aggressive move from Intel</span></div><div class="line" id="191300"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191300" label="[19:13:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the dominance of UEFI is bad enough, but at least CSM permitted 16bit real-mode boot</span></div><div class="line" id="191326"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191326" label="[19:13:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there's no excuse that option got removed</span></div><div class="line" id="191339"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191339" label="[19:13:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's evil</span></div><div class="line" id="191404"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191404" label="[19:14:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it's breaking a major promise of Intel and Microsoft, for &quot;backwards compatibility&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="191545"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191545" label="[19:15:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as a consequence most of the significant progress made by bootstrappable project with x86 is planned for obsolescence and beyond it is an unpreceeded attack against freedom</span></div><div class="line" id="191941"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191941" label="[19:19:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it too affects my approach to revive linux-2.4 with a complete system supported with an alternative unix toolchain on x86</span></div><div class="line" id="191959"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191959" label="[19:19:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because such system would not boot on some recent laptops and whichever intel considers their servers</span></div><div class="line" id="192019"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#192019" label="[19:20:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">uefi is not specific to x86, it also infects arm and riscv</span></div><div class="line" id="192051"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#192051" label="[19:20:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, and even worse, arm and riscv need most recent kernels which are difficult to bring back support with pcc compiler or tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="192117"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#192117" label="[19:21:17]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then all hope in hurd, plan9, inferno</span></div><div class="line" id="192346"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#192346" label="[19:23:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least the 12billion subsidy cashpot for the Intel fab in Magdeburg was terminated</span></div><div class="line" id="205037"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205037" label="[20:50:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I respectfully disagree; choosing 16-bit BIOS as the target for kernel bootstrapping was, in retrospect, a bad choice on our side</span></div><div class="line" id="205128"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205128" label="[20:51:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As it was long destined for removal</span></div><div class="line" id="205357"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205357" label="[20:53:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It is unfortunate that x86 doesn't really provide a &quot;native&quot; boot interface that effectively enables replacing the firmware - but even if it did, I would've considered bootstrapping a UEFI-compatible environment, and going further from there, a priority</span></div><div class="line" id="205445"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205445" label="[20:54:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">UEFI and multiboot are really the only choices for an open interface from the firmware to the kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="205501"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205501" label="[20:55:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, there was also OpenFirmware, but it's essentially extinct</span></div><div class="line" id="205739"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205739" label="[20:57:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ARM and riscv chips usually provide a well-documented (but platform-specific) interface for loading firmware, x86 unfortunately doesn't</span></div><div class="line" id="205845"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205845" label="[20:58:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But depending on such an interface without then bootstrapping a UEFI- or multiboot-compatible stage is problematic precisely because of the nature of such low-level firmware loading interfaces: anything that relies on them becomes, well, firmware.</span></div><div class="line" id="210034"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210034" label="[21:00:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">presence of 16bit real-mode isn't a choice, it's mandatory</span></div><div class="line" id="210055"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210055" label="[21:00:55]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Why?</span></div><div class="line" id="210107"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210107" label="[21:01:07]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Only if you want to retain IBM compatibility.</span></div><div class="line" id="210157"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210157" label="[21:01:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Intel CPUs have been able to boot natively since Sandy Bridge at least; AMD probably a generation later.</span></div><div class="line" id="210212"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210212" label="[21:02:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">x86 real-mode isn't IBM specific, and it's not intel specific either, zilog implemented it too</span></div><div class="line" id="210252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210252" label="[21:02:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That doesn't make it any less obsolete.</span></div><div class="line" id="210309"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210309" label="[21:03:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2024/04/after-48-years-zilog-is-killing-the-classic-standalone-z80-microprocessor-chip/">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2024/04/after-48-years-zilog-is-killing-the-classic-standalone-z80-microprocessor-chip/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="210328"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210328" label="[21:03:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Z80 is not x86</span></div><div class="line" id="210357"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210357" label="[21:03:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And there's, as far as I'm aware, no modern architecture building on its legacy</span></div><div class="line" id="210423"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210423" label="[21:04:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Z8000 could've been, but it's extinct</span></div><div class="line" id="210457"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210457" label="[21:04:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2002/05/12/us/for-parts-nasa-boldly-goes-on-ebay.html">https://www.nytimes.com/2002/05/12/us/for-parts-nasa-boldly-goes-on-ebay.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="210544"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210544" label="[21:05:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;technical difficulties&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="210552"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210552" label="[21:05:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.cbsnews.com/news/boeing-starliner-crew-return-to-earth-delayed-again-nasa-astronauts-butch-wilmore-suni-williams/">https://www.cbsnews.com/news/boeing-starliner-crew-return-to-earth-delayed-again-nasa-astronauts-butch-wilmore-suni-williams/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="210552"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210552" label="[21:05:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Anyway, the goal of bootstrapping is not to get compatibility with 40-year-old systems</span></div><div class="line" id="210645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210645" label="[21:06:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Otherwise riscv would make no sense</span></div><div class="line" id="210646"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210646" label="[21:06:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://itsfoss.com/a-floating-point-error-that-caused-a-damage-worth-half-a-billion/">https://itsfoss.com/a-floating-point-error-that-caused-a-damage-worth-half-a-billion/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="210740"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210740" label="[21:07:40]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">What does that have to do with the need for retaining real mode?</span></div><div class="line" id="212516"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#212516" label="[21:25:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and even if that was bug in Ada, it doesn't mean that other languages (including C) don't have bugs</span></div><div class="line" id="213313"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213313" label="[21:33:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, 16-bit mode is going away</span></div><div class="line" id="213324"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213324" label="[21:33:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's merely hidden</span></div><div class="line" id="213328"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213328" label="[21:33:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I'm sure 32-bit mode will also go away in a few generations</span></div><div class="line" id="213335"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213335" label="[21:33:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for now it might be hidden</span></div><div class="line" id="213347"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213347" label="[21:33:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once they see that world is not broken, they'll remove it</span></div><div class="line" id="213359"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213359" label="[21:33:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a precious space on the silicon wafer</span></div><div class="line" id="213422"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213422" label="[21:34:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you waste it for compatibility, eventually, your competitors who don't keep backwards compatibility will outperform you</span></div><div class="line" id="213455"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213455" label="[21:34:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without x86 real-mode intel hasn't got a bootstrapping path</span></div><div class="line" id="213519"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#213519" label="[21:35:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but how many sales does bootstrapping generate: nothing</span></div><div class="line" id="213521"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213521" label="[21:35:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and almost all other architectures including riscv or arm rely upon it indirectly at least</span></div><div class="line" id="213537"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213537" label="[21:35:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's not &quot;merely hidden&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="213557"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213557" label="[21:35:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">In fact, it's pretty much gone on current CPUs as well, it's just emulated in microcode</span></div><div class="line" id="213639"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213639" label="[21:36:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: to my understanding the entire ISA is routed through some instruction decoder</span></div><div class="line" id="213649"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213649" label="[21:36:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's a whole other concern</span></div><div class="line" id="213815"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213815" label="[21:38:15]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Modern x86 CPUs don't even really start up in 16-bit mode anymore since at least Sandy Bridge - Intel ME actually needs to configure the CPU for 16-bit emulation</span></div><div class="line" id="213825"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213825" label="[21:38:25]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(or AGESA on AMD)</span></div><div class="line" id="213856"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#213856" label="[21:38:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: what's hiding inside the IntelME with it's minix?</span></div><div class="line" id="213937"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213937" label="[21:39:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Intel ME is indeed a whole can of worms; AMD at least made a commitment to replace AGESA with an open-source solution starting with Zen 6</span></div><div class="line" id="214000"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214000" label="[21:40:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can at least cripple Intel ME to some extent</span></div><div class="line" id="214114"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214114" label="[21:41:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but anyway, all this microcode stuff is happening because you vendors can fix some bugs afterwards</span></div><div class="line" id="214143"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214143" label="[21:41:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you make a hardware with bugs, it would be billions of USD to fix it</span></div><div class="line" id="214224"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214224" label="[21:42:24]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Bootstrapping AMD's openSIL will be an interesting challenge :)</span></div><div class="line" id="214253"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214253" label="[21:42:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: but it's not like you can easily interact with your CPU before openSIL would load</span></div><div class="line" id="214313"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214313" label="[21:43:13]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Since it's meant to be open source from the very first instruction that the management cores execute</span></div><div class="line" id="214314"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214314" label="[21:43:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would guess you best bet would be to cross-compile it from some other system that is easier to interact than PC</span></div><div class="line" id="214337"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214337" label="[21:43:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but how do you start bootstrap there?</span></div><div class="line" id="214341"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214341" label="[21:43:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So to get a bootstrap rooted before openSIL, you would need to compile code on management cores</span></div><div class="line" id="214408"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214408" label="[21:44:08]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AMD already supports (in hardware) loading AGESA &amp; UEFI from USB</span></div><div class="line" id="214423"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214423" label="[21:44:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, interesting</span></div><div class="line" id="214449"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214449" label="[21:44:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so more similar to those riscv64 or aaarch64 boards that can load first stage bootloaders from e.g. sdcard or emmc</span></div><div class="line" id="214519"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214519" label="[21:45:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so presumably you would put a stage1 on a USB stick, named as if it were an openSIL+UEFI package, and then boot with the USB boot override pin pulled low (which is a button on the IO shield of most AMD boards these days)</span></div><div class="line" id="214601"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#214601" label="[21:46:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aarch got their separate ATF, at least that's opensource, but hasn't got a bootstrapping path</span></div><div class="line" id="214612"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214612" label="[21:46:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">This is already a required feature on AM5</span></div><div class="line" id="214636"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214636" label="[21:46:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Boot selection pin high -&gt; AGESA loaded from SPI</span></div><div class="line" id="214641"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214641" label="[21:46:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">low -&gt; AGESA loaded from USB</span></div><div class="line" id="214759"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#214759" label="[21:47:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, bootstrapping anything in these pre-firmware environments will be very time-consuming and target specific</span></div><div class="line" id="214835"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214835" label="[21:48:35]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: ATF is different, as it runs on the same Arm cores as the main OS, just in a fully privileged mode</span></div><div class="line" id="214920"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#214920" label="[21:49:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: not sure, i recall some separate cortex-m0 or something exists on various boards, but don't quote me on that</span></div><div class="line" id="214924"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214924" label="[21:49:24]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AGESA &amp; ME are more like the VPU firmware on Raspberry Pi, which starts up the ARM cores from outside</span></div><div class="line" id="214957"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#214957" label="[21:49:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Some ARM systems use a Cortex-M for the same purpose, but it's not ATF that runs on it</span></div><div class="line" id="215034"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215034" label="[21:50:34]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The equivalent on x86 is SMM</span></div><div class="line" id="215041"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215041" label="[21:50:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">generally there are lots of micro CPUs in any hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="215043"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215043" label="[21:50:43]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the equivalent of ATF, that is</span></div><div class="line" id="215105"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215105" label="[21:51:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there are probably even riscv cpus these days there</span></div><div class="line" id="215209"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215209" label="[21:52:09]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Recent RockChip ARM CPUs have several Cortex-M0s in them, but interestingly the first core to boot (from an internal boot ROM) is one of the &quot;little&quot; cores (Cortex-A5x)</span></div><div class="line" id="215311"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215311" label="[21:53:11]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">RK3588 IIRC starts the boot ROM on all 4 A55 cores, but all but one just go into a spin loop, waiting for the main OS to take control of them</span></div><div class="line" id="215345"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215345" label="[21:53:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, tha'ts the main system, but then each periferal would again have auxillary CPUs, e.g. network card</span></div><div class="line" id="215356"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215356" label="[21:53:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or even keyboard I would guess</span></div><div class="line" id="215407"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215407" label="[21:54:07]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then it loads a DDR training stage into SRAM, which starts the &quot;real&quot; RAM, and then loads ATF</span></div><div class="line" id="215443"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215443" label="[21:54:43]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ATF then starts all the &quot;management&quot; cores like the M0s, and finally it loads the main firmware (uboot or EDK2)</span></div><div class="line" id="215443"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215443" label="[21:54:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that's what U-boot's early stages usually do</span></div><div class="line" id="215519"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215519" label="[21:55:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">u-boot tpl or spl are loaded into sram and have ram training code</span></div><div class="line" id="215530"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215530" label="[21:55:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(possibly hardware assisted but I'm not sure)</span></div><div class="line" id="215550"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215550" label="[21:55:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, but RockChip AFAIK uses its own training stage, rather than uboot's</span></div><div class="line" id="215709"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215709" label="[21:57:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think depends on the SoC</span></div><div class="line" id="215721"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215721" label="[21:57:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">u-boot can train some of them, e.g. rk3399</span></div><div class="line" id="215728"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#215728" label="[21:57:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, anything at that point is highly platform-dependent</span></div><div class="line" id="215855"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#215855" label="[21:58:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">after all, UEFI supposedly improved upon x86 real-mode BIOS (however that was implemented, probably ASM during the CP/M era which had some dark history attached of it's creator beaten to death)</span></div><div class="line" id="215950"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#215950" label="[21:59:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess all these are RAM training drivers: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://source.denx.de/u-boot/u-boot/-/tree/master/drivers/ram/rockchip">https://source.denx.de/u-boot/u-boot/-/tree/master/drivers/ram/rockchip</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="220015"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#220015" label="[22:00:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, UEFI standartizes firmware interfaces</span></div><div class="line" id="220034"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#220034" label="[22:00:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whether it is a bit or small standard, that's another thing, but it's better than no standard at all</span></div><div class="line" id="220039"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#220039" label="[22:00:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and everybody doing their own thing</span></div><div class="line" id="220426"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220426" label="[22:04:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">back to IBM, who &quot;standardized&quot; their PC with BIOS, and acquired MS-DOS</span></div><div class="line" id="220604"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220604" label="[22:06:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to comprehend some of the related history, arriving at those z80 systems and vt100 terminals</span></div><div class="line" id="220640"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220640" label="[22:06:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is rather challenging, to re-create some vt100 from schematics fully opensource, both hardware and software</span></div><div class="line" id="220719"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220719" label="[22:07:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, at least x86 real-mode boot did work, for decades</span></div><div class="line" id="220859"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220859" label="[22:08:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the software was written in ASM without excess dependencies, and the hardware could be soldered onto breadboard</span></div><div class="line" id="220923"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#220923" label="[22:09:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;work&quot;, yeah -  see <a rel="nofollow" href="https://aeb.win.tue.nl/linux/kbd/A20.html">https://aeb.win.tue.nl/linux/kbd/A20.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="220930"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#220930" label="[22:09:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">any UEFI system is nowhere near that type of openness, never will</span></div><div class="line" id="221014"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221014" label="[22:10:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">You do realize EDK2, the UEFI reference implementation, is FOSS?</span></div><div class="line" id="221048"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221048" label="[22:10:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: is it written in ASM?</span></div><div class="line" id="221121"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221121" label="[22:11:21]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The low-level parts of it are</span></div><div class="line" id="221144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221144" label="[22:11:44]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But it switches to C code as early as possible</span></div><div class="line" id="221149"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221149" label="[22:11:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is a principle issue, BIOS has to cope with low-level parts, and nothing else</span></div><div class="line" id="221229"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221229" label="[22:12:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in general people don't want to deal with asm</span></div><div class="line" id="221236"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221236" label="[22:12:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">C is easier to read and write</span></div><div class="line" id="221308"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221308" label="[22:13:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even at M2libc level, UEFIs support is almost fully in C, there is very little assembly there</span></div><div class="line" id="221319"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221319" label="[22:13:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(unlike in POSIX part of M2libc)</span></div><div class="line" id="221337"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221337" label="[22:13:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also, it needs to be able to present a unified interface to the operating system, even in the face of the system's hardware changing - otherwise the operating system itself becomes firmware</span></div><div class="line" id="221344"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221344" label="[22:13:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">instead of syscalls, in UEFI you just call a function</span></div><div class="line" id="221501"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221501" label="[22:15:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">This is why Arm's SystemReady initiative is such a big deal - it enables Arm designs to clearly split off the firmware from the OS, which can remain software</span></div><div class="line" id="221529"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221529" label="[22:15:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">enabling users to _install_ an OS, rather than _flash_ one</span></div><div class="line" id="221611"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221611" label="[22:16:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from the standpoint of bootstrapping, this introduces a gigantic circular dependency which cannot be broken</span></div><div class="line" id="221637"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#221637" label="[22:16:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what's the circular dependency?</span></div><div class="line" id="221649"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#221649" label="[22:16:49]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i read backlog, but still a bit confused, lol</span></div><div class="line" id="221656"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221656" label="[22:16:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: this means you need a c++ compiler at a stage of bootstrapping this could not be available</span></div><div class="line" id="221711"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#221711" label="[22:17:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for EDK2?</span></div><div class="line" id="221716"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221716" label="[22:17:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for example</span></div><div class="line" id="221735"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#221735" label="[22:17:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although uboot isn't written in ASM either</span></div><div class="line" id="221810"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221810" label="[22:18:10]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">C is enough</span></div><div class="line" id="221818"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221818" label="[22:18:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nothing forces you to use EDK2 or U-Boot if you bootstrap firmware from the lowest stages (e.g. bootstrap OpenSIL)</span></div><div class="line" id="221901"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221901" label="[22:19:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at that point you are bootstrapping for a very specific target, so unified interface doesn't matter too much</span></div><div class="line" id="222005"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222005" label="[22:20:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">few people would bootstrap their userspace, then fewer people would run kernel bootstrap and then again, almost nobody would be bootstrapping their firmware on a machine</span></div><div class="line" id="222031"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222031" label="[22:20:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the idea is, you use a few custom, platform-specific initial stages written in machine code and hexX, until you get your assembler, then get to a C compiler &amp; shell, and bootstrap just like you started from BIOS (or rather, we are treating BIOS as if we were truly bootstrapping from bare metal; which we really aren't)</span></div><div class="line" id="222058"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222058" label="[22:20:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but if you are really one of those people who want to build up from pure hardware, you'll just figure out how to bring up enough hardware to initialize RAM and I/O</span></div><div class="line" id="222113"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222113" label="[22:21:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then just run your bootstrap form there</span></div><div class="line" id="222122"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222122" label="[22:21:22]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Then, at some later point, you can build the final firmware stages (openSIL, uboot, EDK2, coreboot, etc.), and set them up so that upon reboot, they manage the startup process instead of the full bootstrap chain</span></div><div class="line" id="222132"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222132" label="[22:21:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">exactly</span></div><div class="line" id="222141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222141" label="[22:21:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">We already do this with GRUB</span></div><div class="line" id="222149"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222149" label="[22:21:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so the fact that edk2 or other firmware needs more dependencies, would not block you</span></div><div class="line" id="222240"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222240" label="[22:22:40]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in fact, GRUB IIRC loads the kernel using its EFI stud, effectively implementing a mini-UEFI in the process</span></div><div class="line" id="222246"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222246" label="[22:22:46]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*stub</span></div><div class="line" id="222300"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222300" label="[22:23:00]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">latent byslexia...</span></div><div class="line" id="222340"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222340" label="[22:23:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">u-boot also implements mini-UEFI</span></div><div class="line" id="222400"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222400" label="[22:24:00]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in fact, pretty much a complete and proper UEFI in recent versions</span></div><div class="line" id="222426"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222426" label="[22:24:26]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">albeit not UEFI-PI, just the final interface to the OS</span></div><div class="line" id="222426"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#222426" label="[22:24:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, Linux kernel on my arm machine starts booting with the following in dmesg: [    0.000000] efi: EFI v2.100 by Das U-Boot</span></div><div class="line" id="222501"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222501" label="[22:25:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">UEFI-PI is optional, and not required by the main UEFI standard</span></div><div class="line" id="222509"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222509" label="[22:25:09]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or even by SystemReady</span></div><div class="line" id="223324"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223324" label="[22:33:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: on ARM linux kernel does not need EFI to boot at all, fun one imo</span></div><div class="line" id="223346"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223346" label="[22:33:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with my rockchip system i disabled EFI with both uboot and linux-5.9 kernel, works perfect</span></div><div class="line" id="223400"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223400" label="[22:34:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ly</span></div><div class="line" id="223412"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223412" label="[22:34:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">In that case, you're using the competing firmware interface standard, Multiboot</span></div><div class="line" id="223449"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223449" label="[22:34:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i fiddled together a custom firmware layout, and uboot directly loads kernel, without initrd nor anything</span></div><div class="line" id="223450"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223450" label="[22:34:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which I think Fiwix also uses</span></div><div class="line" id="223516"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223516" label="[22:35:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can't always avoid initrd though</span></div><div class="line" id="223524"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223524" label="[22:35:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with uboot there wasn't any need for a further boot-loader nor multi-boot</span></div><div class="line" id="223526"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223526" label="[22:35:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some drivers don't work well when compiled into the kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="223550"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223550" label="[22:35:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: uboot has 2 standard modes of loading the kernel, UEFI (bootefi) or Multiboot (bootm)</span></div><div class="line" id="223613"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223613" label="[22:36:13]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">plus usually some vendor-specific alternatives</span></div><div class="line" id="223712"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223712" label="[22:37:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder if Fiwix still supports a direct 16-bit boot mode, or only Multiboot</span></div><div class="line" id="223745"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223745" label="[22:37:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but the point is, UEFI and Multiboot are both just interfaces, not tied to any particular implementation from either the firmware or OS side</span></div><div class="line" id="223832"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223832" label="[22:38:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">possibly both but we use multiboot for kexec if I recall correctly</span></div><div class="line" id="223853"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223853" label="[22:38:53]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="223854"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223854" label="[22:38:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">indeed, bootm is not any more direct than bootefi</span></div><div class="line" id="223904"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223904" label="[22:39:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thye both directly load the kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="223925"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#223925" label="[22:39:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just slightly different handover procedure</span></div><div class="line" id="224234"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224234" label="[22:42:34]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, bootm is MultibootV2, while Fiwix is loaded using MultibootV1, which is not compatible</span></div><div class="line" id="224308"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224308" label="[22:43:08]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which is why Fiwix can't load Linux using its implementation of Multiboot kexec</span></div><div class="line" id="224402"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224402" label="[22:44:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is how i load kernel with uboot: bootcmd=mmc dev 1 0;mmc read 0x1f00000 0x13800 0x800;mmc read 0x2080000 0x8000 0xb800;booti 0x2080000 - 0x1f00000</span></div><div class="line" id="224426"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224426" label="[22:44:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without any initrd</span></div><div class="line" id="224429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224429" label="[22:44:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you could also argue that Linux's kexec interface is another firmware interface standard, although no real firmware ever implemented it AFAIK (only the Linux kernel itself)</span></div><div class="line" id="224518"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224518" label="[22:45:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then kernel loads a squashfs identified by a PARTUUID on the mmc MBR</span></div><div class="line" id="224611"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224611" label="[22:46:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the kerne image isn't compressed, but the rootfs is, with a few patches made to kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="224642"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224642" label="[22:46:42]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;booti&quot; is a legacy boot mode AFAIK before either UEFI or Multiboot were standardized on Arm</span></div><div class="line" id="224658"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224658" label="[22:46:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which means, your kernel is probably tightly bound to your particular Arm board</span></div><div class="line" id="224709"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224709" label="[22:47:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">exactly</span></div><div class="line" id="224727"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224727" label="[22:47:27]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you're unlikely to ever successfully &quot;booti&quot; a generic Arm kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="224738"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224738" label="[22:47:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, and i think i removed usb support from uboot too</span></div><div class="line" id="224801"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224801" label="[22:48:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i had to call it quit when digging even lower into ATF</span></div><div class="line" id="224823"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224823" label="[22:48:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That means, your operating system kernel is effectively firmware - hence, by definition, you have an embedded system, not a general purpose one.</span></div><div class="line" id="224855"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224855" label="[22:48:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, i tried to decrease code-duplicate between kernel and uboot as low as possible</span></div><div class="line" id="224904"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224904" label="[22:49:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*ion</span></div><div class="line" id="224942"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224942" label="[22:49:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there shouldn't be anything remaining inside uboot which could do uninentional things with usb or ethernet</span></div><div class="line" id="225010"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225010" label="[22:50:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the &quot;real mode&quot; equivalent seems to be ATF however</span></div><div class="line" id="225024"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225024" label="[22:50:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ARM isn't my peace of cake</span></div><div class="line" id="225037"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225037" label="[22:50:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*piece</span></div><div class="line" id="225059"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225059" label="[22:50:59]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">No, there's no &quot;real mode equivalent&quot; on Arm</span></div><div class="line" id="225138"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225138" label="[22:51:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, and i tried to document an adress mappings table for the rockchip board</span></div><div class="line" id="225141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225141" label="[22:51:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ATF runs in high privileged native mode, basically the same mode builder-hex0 runs in during the x86 bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="225142"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225142" label="[22:51:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and gave up</span></div><div class="line" id="225228"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225228" label="[22:52:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK this is also the default startup mode of recent Intel and AMD cores, before ME/AGESA sets up real mode emulation</span></div><div class="line" id="225252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225252" label="[22:52:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which is when, by default, the main firmware gets control</span></div><div class="line" id="225254"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225254" label="[22:52:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">besides real-mode is tied to a different address-mode, segment+offset addressing scheme, which is rather low-level in comparison to whatever an MMU hides inside</span></div><div class="line" id="225309"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225309" label="[22:53:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for paged memory access</span></div><div class="line" id="225330"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225330" label="[22:53:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">segment addressing is probably emulated in microcode these days</span></div><div class="line" id="225347"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225347" label="[22:53:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(I have no knowledge here)</span></div><div class="line" id="225349"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225349" label="[22:53:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just guessing</span></div><div class="line" id="225351"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225351" label="[22:53:51]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, likely no CPU produced in the last 10 years has any physical segment registers</span></div><div class="line" id="225448"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225448" label="[22:54:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nonetheless, just because an MMU is hiding the details of bank-switching doesn't mean the technique would not exist</span></div><div class="line" id="225451"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225451" label="[22:54:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and is MMU that complex?</span></div><div class="line" id="225509"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225509" label="[22:55:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just a map from one address to another</span></div><div class="line" id="225521"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225521" label="[22:55:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probably, besides IOMMU, io/APIC</span></div><div class="line" id="225536"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225536" label="[22:55:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, current CPUs (since Haswell and Bulldozer) don't even fully emulate real mode</span></div><div class="line" id="225540"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225540" label="[22:55:40]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">notably, Gate A20 is missing</span></div><div class="line" id="225542"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225542" label="[22:55:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i dislike x86_64 for several reasons</span></div><div class="line" id="225623"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#225623" label="[22:56:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gate a20 was coped with for decades, syslinux for example just made sure it's turned on/off, and no further trouble with it</span></div><div class="line" id="225740"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#225740" label="[22:57:40]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so FFFF:0010 and 0000:0000 are different physical addresses, unlike on a real 8086</span></div><br /></div></body></html>